
Outline

I) Define Invasiveness
  A) Is he really going to define "invasive"?  yes he is
    1) Alien
      a) We have this down.  An Occurrence record has a time, a place and a taxon.  This taxon wasn't here last year, it is here this year, == Alien.
    2) Impact
      - This is difficult and covers a massive range of topics: economic, ecological, geo/hydrological, epidemiological...
         None of these are easy to do in DwC, some are *possible*, some might be easy to do with a little nudging here and there...
         So, this talk is to illustrate these with some examples, talk about possible DwC pathways for each one, and just to get folks thinking about the problem.
I.5) Impact: why do we care?
  A) Measuring impact is a crucial ingredient for management planning
    - Cattle Egret (Bubulcus) https://www.iucngisd.org/gisd/speciesname/Bubulcus+ibis
      - invasive in Hawaii
      - rest of U.S. Alien but "seem to have little or no impact"
        - actually *protected* Migratory Bird Treaty Act of 1918 https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-50/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-10/subpart-B/section-10.13
        - existing impact data is not easy to compile 
          - economic impact: https://www.mcagov.org/DocumentCenter/View/1799/CattleEgrets_Gustine-2018 
          - ecological impact: https://tricolor.ice.ucdavis.edu/sites/g/files/dgvnsk3096/files/inline-files/Meese%20CFG%2098%281%29.pdf
    - Barred Owls vs Northern Spotted Owls https://www.fws.gov/project/barred-owl-management
      - Currently a plan in public comment phase to cull ~half a million barred owls in the western US.  Should we invest money into this?
    - Hydrilla risk assessment: https://www.aphis.usda.gov/sites/default/files/hydrilla-verticillata.pdf 
II) Impact: why DwC?
  - EDRR
  - "Invasive" implies impact is known
    - Risk Assessment
      - Already invasive somewhere else
  - What if impact *ISN'T* known
    - EDDMaps, IMapInvasives, SIREN primarily service "known" invasives
    - There is not, to my knowledge, currently a simple technical pathway to report this data.
      1) Impact is noticed anecdotally
      2) Impact pathway is hypothesized and studied in a bespoke study with manual painstaking data collection
        - In what format is this data stored/shared?
      3) Potential for impact can then be inferred from presence data and impact pathways
III) Ecological
  - WNS https://whispers.usgs.gov/home
    - associatedTaxa
    - associatedOccurrences
    - ResourceRelationship
  - crowding out/out-competing
    - presence/absence data only?
  - direct 
    - (lionfish https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/lionfish.html)
    - pythons
      - stomach contents https://data.usgs.gov/datacatalog/data/USGS:630e7961d34e36012ef9f696
  - indirect
    - Hydrilla lowers dissolved oxygen concentrations in water
IV) Economic
  - FL aquatic plants, Water lettuce etc https://plants.ifas.ufl.edu/manage/why-manage-plants/non-native-invasive-plants/
  - Hydrilla interferes with recreational use of water bodies
  - quagga/zebra mussels https://www.ukiahdailyjournal.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/FISHING.jpeg
V) Geo/hydrological
  - Also FL aquatic plants
    - Hydrilla clogs waterways
  - Sporobolus alterniflorus
  - Ammophila arenaria
  - GeoSciML: https://www.ogc.org/standard/geosciml/
VI) Epidemiological
  - a majority of emerging infectious diseases originate in wildlife https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5960580/
  - https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7580882/
  - Mosquitos + Dengue https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aedes_aegypti https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aedes_albopictus
  - co-introduction Python lung parasite in FL
